          "He learned the languages his people brought. German and Czech were his from home. He used English, and he studied enough Italian, French, Spanish, and Irish to hear confessions. The point was the sacrament, available to a penitent in the words he actually spoke. In 1853 he put the Forty Hours devotion on a diocesan rotation, so that the Blessed Sacrament was exposed in one parish after another through the year. Eucharistic prayer of that kind is explained on the [[/blog/eucharistic-adoration/|adoration page]] and in the Church's faith in the [[/blog/eucharist-real-presence/|real presence]]. The rotation made adoration a work of the whole local church.",
