          "Anthony the Great is the father of Christian monastic life in the Egyptian desert. He was born about 251, died in 356, and never received holy orders. Athanasius is careful about that. The man who became the pattern of monks remained a layman, under the Church, present when bishops needed him, and back in his cell when the crowds left. Paul of Thebes is remembered as an earlier hermit, and the Life tells of Anthony visiting him. Pachomius, Anthony's contemporary, organized communities that lived a common rule. Anthony's gift was the cell, the elder, Scripture, work, and psalmody. Benedict, centuries later, would shape the West; his life is on the [[/blog/saint-benedict/|Saint Benedict page]].",
