          "The earliest firm evidence is liturgical, and it is early. The Depositio Martyrum, a Roman list from 354, names Agnes on January 21 and places her in the cemetery on the Via Nomentana. That is a generation after the persecution in which tradition says she died, and it is a public calendar, not a private vision. Pope Damasus, later in the fourth century, had verses cut for her tomb, praising a girl who stood firm. Ambrose, bishop of Milan, writing for consecrated virgins, says she was about twelve and treats her death as a victory already famous. Between those witnesses the Church has enough to preach: a Roman girl, a martyr, a consecrated will, a grave on a known road.",
