        heading: "Reason, religion, and loving-kindness",
        paragraphs: [
          "In 1877 Bosco wrote a short treatise, The Preventive System in the Education of the Young. The repressive system, as he described it, waits to punish after a fall. The preventive system stays close enough to keep the fall from happening. Its pillars are reason, religion, and loving-kindness. Reason means rules that can be explained and a director who is present. Religion means confession and Communion, devotion to Mary, and a climate in which grace is normal. Loving-kindness means a love the young can recognize. He taught that they must know they are loved, and he kept the sacraments strict.",
          "The system was demanding. Bosco cared about purity, about work, about punctual sacraments, and about the company the boys kept. Assistance, in the Salesian sense, means the educator stays in the yard, in the shop, and in the chapel, so that supervision is presence rather than spying. Frequent Communion was his practice in a century when many still approached rarely. He led boys to Communion decades before Pius X's Quam singulari (1910). The [[/blog/eucharist-real-presence/|Eucharist]] and the [[/blog/how-to-go-to-confession/|sacrament of penance]] are the hinges of his method.",
        ],
